 KAFKA:
 Apache Kafka is a distributed event store and stream-processing platform. It is an open-source system developed by the Apache Software Foundation written in Java and Scala. The project aims to provide a unified, high-throughput, low-latency platform for handling real-time data feeds.
 
 why kafka:
 producers
 consumers
 streams
 
 components:
 producers: app that create and send events
 controller/kraft: manages cluster data, controller leadership and cluster coordination
 consumers: app that read and process messages from topics
 kafka connect: integrated data integration framework to quickly and reliable move data into and out of kafka
 kafka streams: a clinet lib for building real time stream processing app
 schema registry: centralized schema management for kafka
 offsets(customer position): tracks the last processes msg for each consumer group so consumers can resume from where they left off
 store messages: brokers persist messages for each partition
 replication: copies each partition across multiple brokers to ensure fault tolerance,high availability, data durability
 
 
 major kafka components:
 producer- creates events
 kafka broker-stores events
 consumers-process events
 
 broker: server that stores topic partitions
 leader replica: handles reads/writes for a partition
 follower replica-copies data
 
 kafka partition strategy:
 partitioning:
 splits a topic into multiple ordered, independent partitions to achieve scalability , parallelism and higher throughput
 
 benefits:
 - scalability
 - parallel processing
 - high throughput
 - fault tolerance
 - ordering
 
 partition strategy:
 key based
 round robin
 custom partitioner
 hash based
 
 consumer group:
 collection of consumer instances that work tohgether to consume and process messages from one or more kafka process
 
 consumer   						Consumer group
 individual application instances	collection of consumer instances
 reads and processes messages		shares processing responsibilities
 can consume multiple partitions	distributes partitions
 
 kafka rebalancing:
 redistributing topic partitions among consumers in a consumer group when the group membership changes(consumer joins,leaves or fails)
 
 rebalancing strategies:
 range assignor-consecutive partitions to consumers
 round robin assigner - partitions distributed evenly
 sticky assigner(default)-keeps existing assignments as much as possible
 
 kafka consumer lag analysis
 the no of unconsumed messages a consumer group is behind for a partition
 
 formula: lag= log end offset(LEO)-committed offset
 
 common causes of high lag:
 - slow processing logic
 - insufficient consumers
 - consumer downtime or frequent restarts
 - long poll/batch configuration
 - resource constraints
 
 how to reduce lag:
 - increase the number of consumer instances
 - optimize processing logic
 - tune consumer configs
 - ensure sufficient resources
 - use backpressure and monitor regularly
 
 kafka delivery gurantees:
 how many times a message is delivered and processes by a consumer
- at-most-once(0 or 1)
possible data loss, no duplicates,low complexity,high performance
- at-least-once
no data loss, possible duplicates, medium complexity,high performance
- exactly-once
no data loss,no duplicates,high complexity, moderate performance(used in any atomic transactions)


